  "sharing": {
    "_what": "What Trillion HQ (the operator account, OPERATOR_ACCOUNT_ID) sees about each customer, and the plain words that say so (src/hq-sharing.mjs keeps and reads it, src/journey.mjs records only what it allows). Two choices per account, kept in APP_KV under `key_prefix` + the account id as { activity, content, set_at, via }. `activity` is what a person did (which screen group, which tool ran, a step of signing up, an error, a report, a notice Trillion sent them: only its kind and title) and is ON until the person turns it off. `content` is what a person wrote or a notice said (a report's quoted words, a notice's body) and is OFF until the person turns it on. An account with no key yet (made before this existed) reads as `default`, so it is counted until it chooses. `via` says how the choice was last set: `sign_up` (the default, written when the account was made), `settings` (the person), or `default` (no key was ever written, so it is only the default being read).",
    "key_prefix": "hq-sharing:",
    "default": {
      "activity": true,
      "content": false
    },
    "words": {
      "sign_up": "Trillion HQ sees what you do here, like which screens you open, which tools run and any errors, so we can fix things for you. We never see what you write unless you allow it. You can change this any time in Settings, under Privacy.",
      "privacy_heading": "What Trillion HQ sees",
      "privacy_lead": "The people who make Trillion look at how each account is used so they can fix what is hard or broken for you. You choose how much they see.",
      "activity_title": "What you do",
      "activity_on": "On: HQ sees which screens you open, which tools your AI runs, any errors, and the reports your AI sends. Never the words in them.",
      "activity_off": "Off: HQ keeps no record of what you do here.",
      "content_title": "What you write",
      "content_on": "On: HQ can also read the words in your reports and notices from Trillion, to understand a problem.",
      "content_off": "Off: HQ never sees the words you wrote or a notice's body. This is the default.",
      "turn_off_activity": "Stop sharing what I do",
      "turn_on_activity": "Share what I do",
      "turn_off_content": "Stop sharing what I write",
      "turn_on_content": "Share what I write",
      "said_activity_off": "HQ no longer sees what you do, and what it kept about your activity is removed.",
      "said_activity_on": "HQ sees what you do again.",
      "said_content_off": "HQ no longer sees what you write.",
      "said_content_on": "HQ can read the words in your reports.",
      "privacy_page": "The people who make Trillion (Trillion HQ) can see, for each account, what the person did here: which screens they opened, which tools their AI ran, errors, the steps of signing up, the notices Trillion sent them (the kind and title) and the reports they or their AI sent us. This is on unless you turn it off in Settings, under Privacy, and turning it off removes what we kept. We never see what you write, such as the words of a report or the body of a notice, unless you allow it there; that is off until you turn it on. What HQ keeps about what you do is removed after 90 days. The overall counts of visits and tool use that Trillion keeps are separate from this choice. Page visits and clicks are not counted at all, in either place, if your browser asks not to be tracked (Do Not Track or Global Privacy Control)."
    }
  },
  "interactions": {
    "_what": "Every visit, click and use of Trillion, counted in one place of its own (src/interactions.mjs admits and counts, src/interaction-log.mjs keeps; never a person's store). A page loads `script_path` (the Worker adds it to every page it serves; gettrillion.ai loads it from the app), which sends batches of events to `collect_path`. An event is self-describing: its `kind` is one row of `kinds`, and the row's `dimensions` are the only facts kept about it; adding a kind of event is one row here. A dimension is filled from the event itself (`from_event`), or by the Worker (`surface`, `device`, `country`, `account`), never trusted from the page when the Worker knows it. No address or browser is kept. A visitor is a hash of the address and browser under a random key made each day and deleted when that day ends (UTC), even when nothing more arrives, so one visitor cannot be followed from one day to the next. Within one day the visitor sketch counts how many, and each raw event keeps the first 6 bytes of its visitor's hash, so a day's events from one visitor can be told apart from another's that day; that day-only mark is kept with the raw events for `keep_raw_days` and then deleted with them, on time even when nothing more arrives. A page whose browser asks not to be tracked (Do Not Track or Global Privacy Control) sends nothing, and the Worker drops what arrives with either header. `surfaces` names where an event happened from its page's host (first match wins); `path_rules` cut each path so no code, file name or person's item is kept (the first rule whose prefix matches; `keep` is how many path parts stay, the rest become `rest_word`). Counts are kept per hour and written once every `flush_seconds`; the raw events of each write are kept together, compressed, for `keep_raw_days`, so any later question can still be asked of them. `screen` is the operator's Visitors screen as DATA (the shared record set with its charts), and `funnel` is the steps from a first visit to paying, each a kind and the facts it must have. A kind's `visitor_scopes` name the facts its different visitors are counted by (a page, an experiment's arm). The same counts feed price experiments (a `price_shown` per visitor and arm, src/price-experiments.mjs `shownCounted`) and any funnel graph whose `measures.interactions` rows name a stage, a kind and the facts it must have (src/funnel-read.mjs). `returns` is who comes back: each row a kind, the surface it happened on and the words for two counts, the accounts counted on a day before the last day read and those of them counted on another day too (read from the accounts counted per day, src/interaction-log.mjs; never a visitor, who is forgotten each day). Look of the screen: one wide chart over time, then the smaller charts, then the pages (Framer Analytics, Mintlify Analytics, Featurebase on Mobbin).",
    "script_path": "/i.js",
    "collect_path": "/i",
    "flush_seconds": 15,
    "flush_at_events": 4000,
    "keep_raw_days": 90,
    "privacy_page": "When a Trillion page loads it counts the visit and what was clicked, by hour. Your IP address and browser are never kept: they are turned into a code under a random key that is made each day and deleted when that day ends (UTC). So we can count how many people visited on a day, and tell which of that day's visits came from the same browser, but not who you are or that you came back on another day. The visit events, with that day-only code, are deleted after {keep_raw_days} days. A browser that asks not to be tracked (Do Not Track or Global Privacy Control) sends nothing.",
    "keep_count_days": 400,
    "read_days": 7,
    "most_read_days": 92,
    "shards": 1,
    "rest_word": ":rest",
    "batch": {
      "most_events": 40,
      "most_bytes": 32768,
      "value_chars": 120,
      "send_every_seconds": 8,
      "session_idle_minutes": 30,
      "breaker": {
        "failures": 5,
        "rest_seconds": 300
      }
    },
